
  # Transactions

  START TRANSACTION;  -- or BEGIN;
  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
  UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
  COMMIT;   -- or ROLLBACK; on failure

  Concept and what it does :-

  START TRANSACTION / COMMIT / ROLLBACK
  Group statements so they succeed or fail atomically

  SAVEPOINT sp1; ... ROLLBACK TO sp1;
  Partial rollback within a larger transaction

  SELECT ... FOR UPDATE;
  Row-level lock — read a row and block others from modifying it until your transaction ends
  (classic pattern for "reserve balance" logic)

  Isolation levels: READ UNCOMMITTED, READ COMMITTED, REPEATABLE READ, SERIALIZABLE
  Trade-off between consistency and concurrency — set with SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL ...

  Idempotency check pattern: INSERT INTO payments (idempotency_key, ...)
  VALUES (?, ...) ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE id = id; (MySQL) or ON CONFLICT (idempotency_key) DO NOTHING (Postgres)
  Insert-or-ignore using a unique constraint — the DB-level backbone of the idempotency work you already do in code

